WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.930
When Avengers: Endgame hits theaters, everyone's
going to be gunning for Thanos, and it's hard

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:08.840
to think of a character who has a bigger score
to settle with the Mad Titan than Thor.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.000
Here's how the God of Thunder could get his
revenge, and take down Thanos once and for

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:13.500
all.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.180
Everyone in the Marvel Cinematic Universe
- or at least the half of it that was still

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.720
breathing after the end of Infinity War - has
a pretty good reason for wanting Thanos dead.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.880
For Thor, though, it's gone beyond just saving
the world.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.900
This time, Thanos made it personal.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.760
The very first thing we see him Thanos do
in Infinity War is obliterate most of the

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.000
remaining Asgardians.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:39.560
It's especially heartbreaking after the character
arc that ran through Thor: Ragnarok, a film

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:44.210
that, despite its comedic moments, was at
heart about Thor learning to let go - of his

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.960
father, of his hammer, and even of Asgard
itself, which he destroyed in order to stop

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:49.460
Hela.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.600
The only consolation was the lesson that he
learned from these trials:

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.500
"Asgard is where our people stand.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.320
Even now, right now, those people need your
help.”

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:06.700
And now, many of those people are dead, including
Loki, the brother that he was just starting

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:07.420
to get along with again.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.680
That's the kind of trauma that results in
a lot of anger, and for Thor, intense anger

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.060
tends to end with someone getting hit very,
very hard.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.750
We don't know how long the gap is between
the end of Infinity War and the beginning

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:25.930
of Endgame, but judging by the trailers, Thor's
had plenty of time to rest up, do some push-ups,

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:26.880
and rethink his strategy.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.640
Of course, that still pits him against the
guy who managed to almost effortlessly take

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.880
out the Hulk, which is pretty impressive.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.680
After all, Hulk's the strongest Avenger, right?

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.040
"Welcome, Strongest Avenger."

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:39.860
"Uh, what?"

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.040
Well, not quite.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:46.680
In the big fight that they have in the Grandmaster's
gladiatorial games, Thor is well on his way

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.390
to a lightning-powered knockout victory.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:53.280
The only reason he loses is due to interference
from the Grandmaster himself.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:58.190
Of course, if this battle happened in a movie
with Hulk's name in the title instead of Thor's,

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:00.540
we might've seen a different outcome, but
still.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.880
The fact that he could level the Hulk with
a single punch, even without his hammer, shows

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.760
just how much force Thor is capable of delivering.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.700
Thor and Thanos are both known by their signature
weapons.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:16.580
Unfortunately for Thanos, we see at the end
of Infinity War that the Infinity Gauntlet

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.080
is burnt out from his infamous finger-snap.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.820
That's a situation that Thor's all too familiar
with himself.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.760
For ages, he wielded the mighty Mjolnir.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:30.100
Unfortunately, it was destroyed in Ragnarok
when Hela fulfilled the prophecies of the

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.220
early '90s and put a stop to Hammer Time.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:33.740
The good news?

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.520
Thor got a brand new mallet called Stormbreaker,
and from what we can tell, it's an improvement.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.500
In addition to having all of the original
Mjolnir's mystical properties, Stormbreaker

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.960
also allows Thor to teleport across the galaxy
by controlling the Bifrost.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.740
Plus, the back half is an axe, so, you know,
it can cut things.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.360
You can't really do that with a hammer.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.780
For anyone else, taking Stormbreaker to the
chest would be a finishing blow.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.200
For Thanos, it's the setup for a one-liner.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.220
"You should've gone for the head."

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:09.380
It's certainly a badass moment for the villain,
but it also raises a pretty important question:

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.210
why didn't Thor go for the head?

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:15.710
He's a seasoned warrior who has smashed his
way through an army of Fire Giants, and he

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.520
knows how to take someone out if he needs.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.200
Why would he even take the chance?

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:24.800
In the movie, that fatal mistake happens because
Thor is so consumed by his desire for revenge,

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:29.450
but it's easy to see this as the kind of memorable
line that begs for a callback.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:34.010
If Thor gets another shot, it's pretty easy
to imagine that he takes Thanos' advice to

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:37.080
heart and just decapitates him without a word.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.300
After all, he's done it before.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:43.600
In the comics, Thanos has been defeated before,
and even died a couple of times.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.410
None of those losses, however, have been quite
as bone-crushingly brutal as the one that

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.060
Thor handed to him back in 2000.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:55.900
In a story from Thor #25, Thanos has turned
his power-hungry attention to a handful of

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:01.800
mystical artifacts: the Illumination Stone,
the Chalice of Ruins, and the Map of All-Ending.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:06.380
All together, they give him the power to potentially
"end all existence."

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:10.771
Things do not look good, and just like we
saw in the films, round one ends with Thor

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.940
on the losing side… but that's not where
the fight ended.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:19.440
Thor makes a comeback over the course of a
six-page slugfest, and winds up beating Thanos

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:25.070
down with Mjolnir over and over until the
Mad Titan can't even stand up, let alone conquer

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:25.880
the universe.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.520
It's almost enough to make you feel bad for
the bad guys.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:29.780
Almost.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.340
There was one additional factor at play, though.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:36.970
In addition to his signature hammer, Thor's
last-minute comeback was aided by a new set

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:41.380
of magic weaponry of his own, and it just
so happens that a few weapons like that already

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:42.600
exist in the MCU.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:50.121
Norse mythology has plenty of intensely destructive
magical weapons, including Gungnir, the Spear

00:04:50.121 --> 00:04:56.270
of Odin; the nearly invulnerable Destroyer
armor; and the Megingjord, a magic belt that

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:57.640
can double Thor's strength.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.220
We even know exactly where that one is!

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:05.080
"We just gotta load Tony's old Hulkbuster
armor, the prototype for Cap’s new shield,

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.060
and the Megin..

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:08.860
The megin…

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:09.640
Thor's magic belt."

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.960
Sure, most Asgardian weapons were probably
destroyed along with Asgard itself at the

00:05:13.962 --> 00:05:18.280
end of Ragnarok, but it's just as likely that
they were scattered around the universe waiting

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:19.760
for someone to gather them up.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:24.610
If things are as desperate as they seem from
all that sad music in the Endgame trailer,

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:27.500
it might be time for Thor to gear up and go
get revenge.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:32.560
Thanos is the kind of threat that requires
you to pull out all the stops - and it sure

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:37.220
does help if "all the stops" includes a magic
spear that will never miss its target.

